<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Latvian Socialist Soviet Republic</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Latvian Socialist Soviet Republic was a Bolshevik state proclaimed in December 1918 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peteris-stucka">Pēteris Stučka</a> and backed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, which took Riga in January 1919. Its rule brought revolutionary tribunals, requisitions and famine in the capital. It collapsed after losing Riga in May 1919 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a> in early 1920, and Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> recognised Latvian independence in August 1920.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/latvian-socialist-soviet-republic.jpg" alt="Latvian Socialist Soviet Republic" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Himasaram · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Latvian_Socialist_Soviet_Republic_(1918%E2%80%931920).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The formation of a Soviet Latvian government was initiated by the Central Committee of the Latvian Social Democracy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, on a proposal by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, at an extraordinary meeting of the party's Russian bureau on 23 November 1918. The republic was proclaimed on 17 December 1918 with the political, economic and military backing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> and the Bolshevik government of the Russian SFSR. Pēteris Stučka was head of government, with Jūlijs Daniševskis as his deputy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its armed forces, made up of the Red Latvian Riflemen and other units of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, quickly took most of the territory of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>. They pushed the provisional government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlis-ulmanis">Kārlis Ulmanis</a> into a small pocket around the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Liepāja</a>. The 1st Joint Congress of Workers', Landless and Riflemen's Councils met in Riga on 1 January 1919, announced the republic, declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/proletariat">proletariat</a> and elected a Latvian Central Executive Committee of 60 members.</p><p>Stučka's government introduced sweeping communist reforms. Some, such as the expropriation of the property of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourgeoisie">bourgeoisie</a>, were at first popular. The unilateral nationalisation of all agrarian land had dire economic consequences for the cities, because rural support fell sharply and peasants would no longer supply townspeople with food on the government's terms. Shortages became critical and people in Riga and other cities began to starve. A wave of Red Terror followed in town and country, aimed at alleged counter-revolutionaries blamed for the regime's failures. Arbitrary revolutionary tribunals and the so-called Flintenweiber, or gun-women, were part of it.</p><p>In the spring of 1919 the Ulmanis government, backed by the Entente and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freikorps">German Freikorps</a> units, counter-attacked and quickly regained the lost territory. It retook Riga on 22 May 1919. The republic was reduced to part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latgale">Latgale</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, until its final defeat in the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daugavpils">Daugavpils</a>, by combined Latvian and Polish forces, in early 1920.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Latvian Central Executive Committee appointed eleven members of the government, or Council of Commissars. Stučka was chairman and also commissar of justice, and Kārlis Pētersons was commissar of war. All members of the government also sat on the presidium of the committee and on the central committee of the Latvian Social Democracy, later the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latvia">Latvia</a>, so power rested with a narrow circle. Eight economic commissariats were merged into a People's Economic Council, and the commissariats for war, internal affairs and justice into a Revolutionary War Council. Soviet historiography later treated the Soviet occupation of Latvia in 1940 as the restoration of power and the independence of 1920 to 1940 as a temporary break.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Latvian Socialist Soviet Republic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Within weeks the Red Army invaded and the <b>Latvian Socialist Soviet Republic</b> of Pēteris Stučka took Riga in January 1919, leaving the national government a foothold around Liepāja.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-latvia">Republic of Latvia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
